1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,297
We are team Nga Chain AI Layer Two. This is
Promise Ops, our working MVP for

2
00:00:05,297 --> 00:00:10,524
protecting the original delivery promise.
All data in this demonstration is

3
00:00:10,524 --> 00:00:11,221
fictional.

4
00:00:14,172 --> 00:00:19,275
It is nine in the morning at Stockholm
South. Each order follows four controls:

5
00:00:19,275 --> 00:00:24,702
pick, dispatch cutoff, last viable dispatch,
and the promised delivery time. The red

6
00:00:24,702 --> 00:00:25,671
line marks now.

7
00:00:26,282 --> 00:00:31,861
The yellow controls simulate feeds and time.
The rules, ownership checks and outcome

8
00:00:31,861 --> 00:00:34,517
calculations run inside the application.

9
00:00:34,688 --> 00:00:36,523
Let us move forward thirty minutes.

10
00:00:39,704 --> 00:00:45,002
Two orders have missed their pick milestone.
Order S E ten forty two comes first

11
00:00:45,002 --> 00:00:50,499
because business deliveries win ties. Its
original promise is two in the afternoon.

12
00:00:51,094 --> 00:00:55,491
The warehouse feed has been read after the
milestone. That makes the missing

13
00:00:55,491 --> 00:00:57,921
confirmation actionable. We open the case.

14
00:01:00,516 --> 00:01:05,516
Anna takes ownership. The system records her
response and stops the fifteen minute

15
00:01:05,516 --> 00:01:06,553
escalation timer.

16
00:01:09,125 --> 00:01:14,064
An expedite uses warehouse capacity. The
coordinator cannot approve it, so Anna

17
00:01:14,064 --> 00:01:17,253
records the reason and escalates to the
shift lead.

18
00:01:21,141 --> 00:01:25,472
We switch to the shift lead. This role has
permission to record an expedite.

19
00:01:26,079 --> 00:01:31,075
Erik allocates a picker, records the
evidence, and logs a ten euro intervention

20
00:01:31,075 --> 00:01:31,391
cost.

21
00:01:35,579 --> 00:01:40,925
The outcome is recorded. The activity log
preserves who owned, escalated and decided

22
00:01:40,925 --> 00:01:43,280
the case, with the evidence and cost.

23
00:01:46,422 --> 00:01:51,713
Next, we return to the coordinator and
investigate order S E ten fifty one.

24
00:01:56,250 --> 00:02:01,171
The picker confirms that the goods were
packed before the milestone. The handheld

25
00:02:01,171 --> 00:02:05,423
was offline. Anna closes this as a false
alarm, with written evidence.

26
00:02:11,516 --> 00:02:15,962
What happens if the warehouse feed goes
quiet? We pause it and advance forty five

27
00:02:15,962 --> 00:02:16,401
minutes.

28
00:02:23,391 --> 00:02:28,435
The stale feed warning is visible. New
absence based alerts pause, uncertain

29
00:02:28,435 --> 00:02:32,351
controls are marked unknown, and a data
quality task opens.

30
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:37,407
We resume the feed and advance five minutes.
The backlog arrives before the rules

31
00:02:37,407 --> 00:02:38,653
evaluate the orders.

32
00:02:43,750 --> 00:02:49,617
Order S E ten seventy seven now has a
genuine dispatch problem. It is picked, but
no

33
00:02:49,617 --> 00:02:51,643
vehicle is available in time.

34
00:02:51,625 --> 00:02:56,852
Anna takes ownership and records a handoff
to customer service, with the expected

35
00:02:56,852 --> 00:03:02,078
arrival time. This records the handoff; the
MVP does not send a customer message.

36
00:03:12,016 --> 00:03:15,984
We move to Orders and advance five hours to
see what actually happened.

37
00:03:19,032 --> 00:03:23,898
Order ten forty two arrived at twelve thirty
two, before its two o clock promise.

38
00:03:23,898 --> 00:03:28,163
Picking and dispatch were late, but the
customer promise was protected.

39
00:03:28,735 --> 00:03:34,111
Order ten seventy seven arrived at fourteen
thirty five. Informing the customer does

40
00:03:34,111 --> 00:03:38,463
not change the original promise: this
delivery still counts as late.

41
00:03:39,141 --> 00:03:43,917
Outcomes closes the loop. We measure against
the original promise, which cannot be

42
00:03:43,917 --> 00:03:44,325
edited.

43
00:03:44,329 --> 00:03:49,555
In this simulated morning, ninety three
point three percent of orders are on time,

44
00:03:49,555 --> 00:03:54,526
and one late delivery was prevented. The ten
euro action cost remains visible.

45
00:03:55,094 --> 00:04:00,120
The twenty five euro value is a business
case assumption, not a measured saving.

46
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:05,271
Three alerts are also too few to judge the
pilot targets. The scorecard makes that

47
00:04:05,271 --> 00:04:06,465
limitation visible.

48
00:04:06,516 --> 00:04:11,502
Now we replay the same morning without
intervention. We reset and advance beyond
the

49
00:04:11,502 --> 00:04:12,511
delivery promise.

50
00:04:21,688 --> 00:04:26,795
Without action, order ten forty two arrives
at fourteen twenty five. On time

51
00:04:26,795 --> 00:04:31,834
delivery falls to eighty six point seven
percent. This is a controlled demo

52
00:04:31,834 --> 00:04:34,253
comparison, not a production result.

53
00:04:34,922 --> 00:04:39,146
The interface also supports Vietnamese,
including system messages.

54
00:04:39,766 --> 00:04:42,241
We return to English for the final overview.

55
00:04:42,844 --> 00:04:47,847
Since Round One, the milestone rule has
gained a read after milestone check. It

56
00:04:47,847 --> 00:04:53,039
leaves time to act while avoiding alerts
based on an unread feed. The demo harness

57
00:04:53,039 --> 00:04:56,839
and role switch stand in for real feeds and
company sign in.

58
00:04:56,907 --> 00:05:02,481
Under the hood, one Fast API service
combines ingestion, rules, workflow and read

59
00:05:02,481 --> 00:05:07,849
models with a SQLite database. It reads
source events and never writes back to

60
00:05:07,849 --> 00:05:09,707
warehouse or order systems.

61
00:05:09,735 --> 00:05:14,894
The pilot will test these rules and collect
labelled outcomes for later modelling.

62
00:05:14,894 --> 00:05:19,612
Today, the MVP demonstrates ownership,
decision rights, evidence and honest

63
00:05:19,612 --> 00:05:23,324
measurement against the promise originally
made. Thank you.
